Issues.Body:
### Command

build (also serve)

### Is this a regression?

Yes. The previous version in which this bug was not present was **21.2.27** (webpack 5.105.2). Strictly speaking, 21.2.27 already emits the bad import too (see below). webpack 5.112 just makes it an error.

### Description

With `@angular-devkit/build-angular:browser` 21.2.28 (which updated webpack to 5.112.0), an AOT build fails with:

```
./src/app/data.service.ts:1:0-33 - Error: export 'Status' (imported as 'Status') was not found in './types' (module has no exports)
```

`Status` is an interface. The failure needs all three of these in one file:

1. The file has a decorated class, so `removeIvyJitSupportCalls` removes its `ɵsetClassMetadata` call and runs `elideImports`.
2. One `import { ... }` statement brings in several interfaces.
3. Some of those interfaces are used only in type annotations, and at least one is either re-exported (`export type { Status }`) or used in an interface `extends Status`.

What happens:

- `elideImports` drops the annotation-only specifiers. Its visitor skips `TypeReferenceNode`s.
- It keeps the others as "used". It records `ExportSpecifier`s without checking for `export type`, and it only skips `implements` heritage clauses, so an interface's `extends` counts as a use.
- That rewrites the `ImportDeclaration`. TypeScript then skips its own import elision for that statement, because `isElisionBlocked` is true when a `before` transformer has replaced the node.
- So `import { Status } from './types'` survives into the emitted JS, although `Status` has no runtime value.

With webpack 5.105 this was a silent dead import. The repro's dev build on 21.2.27 still contains `__webpack_require__(/*! ./types */ ...)` in `data.service`'s module.

webpack 5.112 now link-checks import specifiers even when they are never read, and Angular's `strictExportPresence: true` makes that an error.

Controls (each of these builds cleanly on 21.2.28):

- `import type { Item, Status }`
- no `export type { Status }`, so both names are annotation-only and the whole import is removed
- `import { Status }` on its own line plus `export type { Status }`, so nothing in that statement is partially removed and TypeScript elides it as usual
- the same file without the `@Injectable` decorator, so `elideImports` never runs

A possible fix is for `elideImports` to count a symbol as used only when its alias target has a value meaning (`SymbolFlags.Value`). Narrower alternatives: skip `export type` declarations and specifiers, and skip interface heritage clauses.

### Minimal Reproduction

Seven files. `npm install`, then `npx ng build` (or `npx ng build -c development`).

`package.json`
```json
{
  "name": "ngtools-webpack-type-reexport-repro",
  "private": true,
  "dependencies": {
    "@angular/common": "21.2.25",
    "@angular/compiler": "21.2.25",
    "@angular/core": "21.2.25",
    "@angular/platform-browser": "21.2.25",
    "rxjs": "7.8.2",
    "tslib": "2.8.1"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@angular-devkit/build-angular": "21.2.28",
    "@angular/cli": "21.2.28",
    "@angular/compiler-cli": "21.2.25",
    "typescript": "5.9.3"
  }
}
```

`angular.json`
```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "projects": {
    "repro": {
      "projectType": "application",
      "root": "",
      "sourceRoot": "src",
      "architect": {
        "build": {
          "builder": "@angular-devkit/build-angular:browser",
          "options": {
            "outputPath": "dist/repro",
            "index": "src/index.html",
            "main": "src/main.ts",
            "tsConfig": "tsconfig.json"
          },
          "configurations": {
            "production": { "optimization": true, "outputHashing": "all" },
            "development": { "optimization": false, "sourceMap": true }
          },
          "defaultConfiguration": "production"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

`tsconfig.json`
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ES2022",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "dom"],
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "files": ["src/main.ts"]
}
```

`src/index.html`
```html
<!doctype html>
<html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>repro</title></head><body><app-root></app-root></body></html>
```

`src/main.ts`
```ts
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { AppComponent } from './app/app.component';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent).catch(err => console.error(err));
```

`src/app/app.component.ts`
```ts
import { Component, inject } from '@angular/core';
import { DataService } from './data.service';

@Component({ selector: 'app-root', template: '{{ id }}' })
export class AppComponent {
  id = inject(DataService).first().id;
}
```

`src/app/types.ts`
```ts
export interface Item {
  id: number;
}

export interface Status {
  done: boolean;
}
```

`src/app/data.service.ts`
```ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { Item, Status } from './types';

// Re-export a type for consumers of this service.
export type { Status };

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class DataService {
  first(): Item {
    return { id: 1 };
  }
}
```

The `extends` variant fails the same way. Replace the `export type { Status };` line with:

```ts
export interface DetailedStatus extends Status {
  note: string;
}
```

### Exception or Error

```
./src/app/data.service.ts:1:0-33 - Error: export 'Status' (imported as 'Status') was not found in './types' (module has no exports)
```

### Your Environment

```
Angular CLI       : 21.2.28
Angular           : 21.2.25
Node.js           : 20.20.2
Package Manager   : npm 10.8.2
Operating System  : darwin arm64

@angular-devkit/build-angular  21.2.28
@angular/cli                   21.2.28
@angular/common                21.2.25
@angular/compiler              21.2.25
@angular/compiler-cli          21.2.25
@angular/core                  21.2.25
@angular/platform-browser      21.2.25
rxjs                           7.8.2
typescript                     5.9.3
webpack                        5.112.0
```

Also reproduced on Node 22.23.2 (node:22-alpine) in the real app.

### Anything else relevant?

- We hit this in a production app when moving from build-angular 21.2.26 to 21.2.28. 7 interface names in 3 files failed, and both `ng build` and `ng serve` broke.
- Workaround: `import type`.
- Neither TypeScript guard catches it. `isolatedModules` doesn't flag the pattern, and `verbatimModuleSyntax` flags every type import in a large codebase.
- `ngc` alone emits these files correctly. Only the `@ngtools/webpack` pipeline leaves the specifier behind.
- The `@angular/build` (esbuild) builders are not affected, as far as we can tell.
